insert into questions (id, unit, topic, concept, stem, choices, answer, why) values
  ('D01', 'Derivatives', 'Derivative Markets and Instruments', 'Derivative definition', 'A derivative is a financial instrument whose value is best described as:', '["Fixed by contract and unrelated to any other asset''s price","Derived from the value of an underlying asset, rate, or index","Determined solely by the creditworthiness of its issuer"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A derivative''s defining feature is that its value is contractually linked to, and derived from, an underlying asset, rate, or index such as a stock, bond, currency, or interest rate. Describing its value as fixed by contract confuses the payoff formula, which is fixed, with the value itself, which moves as the underlying moves, and issuer creditworthiness affects counterparty risk, not the instrument''s fundamental value driver.'),
  ('D02', 'Derivatives', 'Derivative Markets and Instruments', 'Exchange-traded vs OTC', 'Compared with an over-the-counter (OTC) derivative, an exchange-traded derivative is best characterized by:', '["Contract terms privately negotiated between the two counterparties","Standardized contract terms and a central clearinghouse that guarantees performance","Little to no regulatory oversight of trading activity"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Exchange-traded derivatives have standardized terms (size, expiration, strike) and a clearinghouse that becomes the counterparty to every trade, virtually eliminating counterparty default risk. Privately negotiated terms describe an OTC contract instead, which is customized to the two parties'' needs but carries the counterparty risk that a clearinghouse removes; exchange trading is also among the more heavily regulated venues, not less.'),
  ('D03', 'Derivatives', 'Derivative Markets and Instruments', 'Hedging vs speculation', 'An airline that consumes large amounts of jet fuel enters into a derivative contract to lock in a future fuel purchase price. This use of derivatives is best classified as:', '["Arbitrage, since the airline is exploiting a mispricing between fuel markets","Hedging, since the airline is reducing its exposure to rising fuel prices","Speculation, since the airline is taking on new price risk it did not have before"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The airline already has fuel-price exposure from its ordinary operations, so locking in a future price reduces existing risk, which is the definition of hedging. This is not speculation, which involves taking on new risk in pursuit of profit that a party would not otherwise have, and it is not arbitrage, which requires a riskless profit from a pricing discrepancy rather than risk reduction.'),
  ('D04', 'Derivatives', 'Derivative Markets and Instruments', 'Leverage in derivatives', 'A futures contract provides exposure to $60,000 of an equity index for an initial margin deposit of $6,000. If the index rises by 4%, the percentage return earned on the margin deposit posted is closest to:', '["40%","10%","4%"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The dollar gain on the underlying exposure is 4% x 60,000 = $2,400, and that gain applies against the much smaller $6,000 margin actually posted, for a return of 2,400/6,000 = 40%, the leverage effect that makes derivatives capital-efficient. Reporting 10% mistakes the 10-to-1 notional-to-margin leverage ratio itself for the percentage return, and reporting 4% ignores leverage altogether, as if the full notional value had been paid in cash.'),
  ('D05', 'Derivatives', 'Derivative Markets and Instruments', 'Arbitrage definition', 'A trader simultaneously buys an asset in one market at $100 and sells an identical, perfectly substitutable asset in another market at $101, locking in the $1 difference with no net cash outlay and no risk. This activity is best described as:', '["Speculation, because the trader is exposed to future price movements","Arbitrage, because it earns a riskless profit from a temporary price discrepancy with no net investment","Hedging, because the trader is reducing an existing risk exposure"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Buying low and simultaneously selling an identical asset high, with no net investment and no risk, is the textbook definition of arbitrage, and the trades this activity generates are exactly what pushes the two prices back together. It is not speculation, since speculation requires bearing price risk in hopes of profit rather than locking in a riskless spread, and it is not hedging, since the trader has no pre-existing position being offset.'),
  ('D06', 'Derivatives', 'Derivative Markets and Instruments', 'Forward vs contingent claim', 'Relative to a forward commitment, a contingent claim such as an option is distinguished by which feature?', '["Both parties are obligated to perform at settlement regardless of the underlying''s price","Its value never depends on the price of an underlying asset","The payoff is asymmetric, since the option holder can walk away from an unfavorable outcome"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A contingent claim''s payoff is asymmetric because the holder has the right, but not the obligation, to exercise, so losses are capped at the premium paid while gains can grow with the underlying. Describing both parties as obligated to perform describes a forward commitment instead, where both sides must perform regardless of price, and claiming value never depends on an underlying is false of both instrument types, since forwards and options alike derive their value from an underlying asset.'),
  ('D07', 'Derivatives', 'Derivative Markets and Instruments', 'Hedging vs speculation vs arbitrage', 'Three traders each execute one transaction. Trader X buys a stock she believes is undervalued, fully bearing the risk that she is wrong. Trader Y, who owns a bond portfolio, sells bond futures to offset the risk of rising interest rates. Trader Z buys an identical government bond simultaneously in two markets at two different prices with no net cash outlay, locking in a riskless spread. Which trader is engaged in arbitrage?', '["Trader X, because she is committing capital to a directional view","Trader Y, because she is using a derivative instrument to manage risk","Trader Z, because she is exploiting a price discrepancy with no net investment and no risk"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Trader Z''s simultaneous purchase and sale of the identical bond at two different prices, with no net cash outlay and no risk, is the defining pattern of arbitrage. Trader X is speculating, since she bears directional risk in pursuit of profit, and Trader Y is hedging, since she is offsetting an existing interest-rate exposure with a derivative rather than exploiting a mispricing; both are legitimate derivative uses, but neither is arbitrage.'),
  ('D08', 'Derivatives', 'Derivative Markets and Instruments', 'Counterparty risk OTC vs exchange', 'A key risk that is present in an OTC forward contract but virtually eliminated in an exchange-traded futures contract is:', '["Market risk, since only OTC contracts are exposed to price movements in the underlying","Liquidity risk, since futures contracts can never be closed out before expiration","Counterparty (credit) risk, since a clearinghouse guarantees performance on every exchange-traded contract"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Because a clearinghouse interposes itself as the buyer to every seller and seller to every buyer on an exchange, and requires daily margining, the risk that the other side defaults is virtually eliminated for futures but remains a real concern in a bilateral OTC forward. Market risk from the underlying''s price movement is present in both instrument types, and futures are generally more liquid than forwards, easily closed out before expiration by an offsetting trade, not less.'),
  ('D09', 'Derivatives', 'Forward Commitments', 'Forward contract mechanics', 'In a forward contract, the party with the long position:', '["Has the right, but not the obligation, to buy the underlying at the forward price","Is obligated to buy the underlying asset at the forward price at the contract''s expiration","Pays a premium at initiation for the right to participate in the contract"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A forward contract obligates the long position to buy, and the short position to sell, the underlying at the agreed forward price at expiration, regardless of where the spot price ends up. Describing a right rather than an obligation instead describes an option, and forwards require no premium at initiation, since the forward price is set precisely so that the contract has zero value to either party at the start.'),
  ('D10', 'Derivatives', 'Forward Commitments', 'Long forward payoff', 'An investor enters a long forward contract on a stock with a forward price of $75. At expiration, the stock''s spot price is $82. The payoff to the long position is:', '["-$7","$7","$82"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The payoff to a long forward at expiration is S_T - F = 82 - 75 = $7, a gain, because the long position is obligated to pay only $75 for a stock now worth $82. The -$7 figure reverses the subtraction, which is instead the payoff to the short position, and $82 simply restates the spot price without netting out the forward price the long side must pay to acquire it.'),
  ('D11', 'Derivatives', 'Forward Commitments', 'Short forward payoff', 'An investor enters a short forward contract on a bond with a forward price of $1,020. At expiration, the bond''s spot price is $1,050. The payoff to the short position is:', '["$30","-$30","$1,020"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The payoff to a short forward at expiration is F - S_T = 1,020 - 1,050 = -$30, a loss, because the short side must deliver a bond worth more than the fixed price it receives. The $30 figure reverses the subtraction, which is instead the payoff to the long position in this same contract, and $1,020 simply restates the forward price with no netting against the spot price at all.'),
  ('D12', 'Derivatives', 'Forward Commitments', 'Forwards vs futures', 'Relative to a forward contract, a futures contract on the same underlying is most likely to feature:', '["Standardized contract terms, daily marking-to-market, and a clearinghouse guarantee","Fully customized terms negotiated privately between the two original counterparties","A single cash settlement at expiration with no interim cash flows"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Futures are standardized by the exchange (contract size, expiration, deliverable grade), settled daily through marking-to-market with gains and losses moving through a margin account, and guaranteed by a clearinghouse. Customized, privately negotiated terms describe a forward instead, and a forward, not a futures contract, is the one with a single settlement at expiration and no interim cash flows, since it is not marked to market along the way.'),
  ('D13', 'Derivatives', 'Forward Commitments', 'Futures margin mechanics', 'In futures trading, the initial margin is best described as:', '["A performance bond deposited when the position is opened, sized well below the contract''s full notional value","A one-time fee paid to the exchange that is never returned to the trader","The full notional value of the contract, posted in cash to guarantee delivery"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Initial margin is a good-faith performance bond, typically a small fraction of the contract''s notional value, that the trader deposits to open a futures position and can withdraw, subject to the maintenance margin floor, as the account is marked to market. It is not a fee to the exchange, since it remains the trader''s own equity in the account, and it is far smaller than the full notional value, which is exactly what gives futures their leverage.'),
  ('D14', 'Derivatives', 'Forward Commitments', 'Mark-to-market variation margin', 'An investor holds a long futures position on 500 units of a commodity. The futures settlement price rises from $40.00 to $41.20 over the trading day. The variation margin credited to the investor''s account is:', '["$600","$60","$20,600"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Daily mark-to-market credits the change in settlement price times the contract''s unit size: (41.20 - 40.00) x 500 = $1.20 x 500 = $600 credited to this long position. The $60 figure misplaces a decimal in the price change, treating it as $0.12 rather than $1.20, and $20,600 reports the day''s ending notional value (41.20 x 500) rather than the day''s gain, confusing the position''s total worth with how much it moved.'),
  ('D15', 'Derivatives', 'Forward Commitments', 'Margin call mechanics', 'A trader opens a long futures position by posting an initial margin of $3,000; the maintenance margin is $2,200. Each $1 decline in the futures price reduces the account''s value by $250. Over the next two trading days, the futures price falls by $3 on day 1 and by $1 on day 2. Which statement is correct?', '["No margin call is triggered, because the two-day cumulative loss of $1,000 is smaller than the $3,000 initial margin posted","A margin call is triggered after day 2, and the trader must deposit $1,000 to restore the account to the $3,000 initial margin level","A margin call is triggered after day 2, and the trader must deposit $200 to restore the account to the $2,200 maintenance margin level"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Day 1''s $3 decline costs 3 x $250 = $750, leaving a balance of $2,250, still above the $2,200 maintenance margin, so no call yet. Day 2''s $1 decline costs another $250, dropping the balance to $2,000, which breaches maintenance margin and triggers a call; the deposit required restores the account all the way to the $3,000 initial margin, or $1,000, not merely up to the $2,200 maintenance floor. Comparing the $1,000 cumulative loss to the $3,000 initial margin ignores that a call is triggered the moment the running balance dips below maintenance margin, and topping up only to the maintenance level is the single most common misunderstanding of how a margin call is cured.'),
  ('D16', 'Derivatives', 'Forward Commitments', 'Futures liquidity', 'A key practical advantage of a standardized, exchange-traded futures contract over a customized OTC forward is that the futures contract:', '["Can be tailored precisely to the hedger''s exact quantity and delivery date","Never requires the posting of margin or collateral","Is generally easier to close out before expiration by entering an offsetting trade"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Because futures contracts are standardized and trade on an exchange with many participants, a trader can typically close out a position before expiration simply by entering an equal and opposite trade, giving futures greater liquidity than most OTC forwards. Precise tailoring to an exact quantity or date is a forward''s advantage, not a futures contract''s, since standardization is what sacrifices that customization, and futures, not forwards, are the ones that require ongoing margin to be posted.'),
  ('D17', 'Derivatives', 'Forward Commitments', 'Marking-to-market purpose', 'The primary purpose of daily marking-to-market on a futures position is to:', '["Determine the final cash settlement amount, which is only calculated once at expiration","Allow the exchange to collect a transaction fee based on the day''s price change","Realize gains and losses each day, limiting the buildup of counterparty credit exposure"]'::jsonb, 2, 'By settling gains and losses through the margin account at the end of each trading day, marking-to-market prevents losses from accumulating unrealized over the life of the contract, which is what keeps counterparty credit exposure small and protects the clearinghouse guarantee. It is not a one-time, at-expiration calculation, since that description applies to a forward contract''s single settlement, and the daily cash flow is a gain or loss credited or debited to the trader''s own account, not a fee collected by the exchange.'),
  ('D18', 'Derivatives', 'Forward Commitments', 'Long forward payoff (FX)', 'A company enters a long forward contract to buy 100,000 euros at a forward rate of $1.08 per euro. At expiration, the spot rate is $1.05 per euro. The payoff to the company''s forward position is:', '["-$3,000","$3,000","$105,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The payoff to a long forward is (S_T - F) times the contract size: (1.05 - 1.08) x 100,000 = -$0.03 x 100,000 = -$3,000, because the company is obligated to pay $1.08 per euro for a currency now worth only $1.05. The $3,000 figure drops the negative sign, reversing which side of the trade gains, and $105,000 simply restates the total spot value of the euros (1.05 x 100,000) without netting out the $1.08 forward price actually owed.'),
  ('D19', 'Derivatives', 'Forward Commitments', 'Forward hedge effective price', 'A refiner needs to buy 1,000 barrels of oil in three months and enters a long forward contract at a forward price of $72 per barrel to hedge the purchase. In three months, the spot price of oil is $79 per barrel. Accounting for both the forward payoff and the cost of buying the oil at the prevailing spot price, the refiner''s net effective cost per barrel is:', '["$79 per barrel, since the forward gain simply offsets the exchange''s transaction fee","$72 per barrel, since the $7 per barrel gain on the forward offsets the higher spot price paid for the oil","$65 per barrel, since the forward gain of $7 is subtracted from the spot price twice"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The long forward gains S_T - F = 79 - 72 = $7 per barrel, and that gain offsets the extra $7 per barrel the refiner must pay to buy oil at the now-higher $79 spot price, leaving a net effective cost of 79 - 7 = $72 per barrel, exactly the forward price locked in three months earlier, which is the entire point of the hedge. Reporting $79 ignores the forward payoff entirely, as if no hedge existed, and $65 subtracts the $7 gain a second time, effectively double-counting it.'),
  ('D20', 'Derivatives', 'Contingent Claims', 'Call vs put definition', 'Which statement correctly distinguishes a call option from a put option?', '["A call obligates the holder to sell the underlying; a put obligates the holder to buy it","A call and a put both obligate the holder to transact at the strike price at expiration","A call gives the holder the right to buy the underlying at the strike price; a put gives the holder the right to sell it"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A call option gives its holder the right, but not the obligation, to buy the underlying at the strike price, while a put option gives its holder the right to sell at the strike price; in both cases the writer bears the obligation if the holder chooses to exercise. Reversing which side gets to buy versus sell describes the buy and sell rights backwards, and describing a mutual obligation to transact describes a forward commitment, not an option, which the holder can simply decline to exercise if unfavorable.'),
  ('D21', 'Derivatives', 'Contingent Claims', 'Moneyness (call)', 'A call option has a strike price of $50. The underlying stock currently trades at $46. This call option is:', '["Out-of-the-money, because the strike price exceeds the current stock price","In-the-money, because immediate exercise would produce a positive payoff","At-the-money, because the strike and stock price are close to each other"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A call is out-of-the-money whenever the strike price exceeds the current stock price, since exercising immediately (buying at $50 a stock worth only $46) would produce a loss, not a gain, so no rational holder would exercise. In-the-money would instead require the stock price to exceed the strike, and at-the-money requires the two to be equal; a $4 gap in this direction is squarely out-of-the-money, not merely close.'),
  ('D22', 'Derivatives', 'Contingent Claims', 'Intrinsic value vs time value', 'A call option with a strike price of $40 is trading for a premium of $6.50 when the underlying stock price is $43. The time value of this option is:', '["$3.50","$6.50","$3.00"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Intrinsic value is max(0, S - X) = 43 - 40 = $3.00, and time value is whatever remains of the premium once intrinsic value is subtracted out: 6.50 - 3.00 = $3.50. The $6.50 figure reports the full premium without separating out the intrinsic portion at all, and $3.00 reports the intrinsic value itself, mistaking it for the time value it is supposed to be subtracted from.'),
  ('D23', 'Derivatives', 'Contingent Claims', 'Long call payoff', 'An investor holds a long call option with a strike price of $60. At expiration, the underlying stock is trading at $68. The payoff to the long call is:', '["$8","$0","-$8"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The payoff to a long call at expiration is max(0, S_T - X) = max(0, 68 - 60) = $8, since the holder can buy at $60 a stock worth $68. Reporting $0 would only be correct if the stock had finished at or below the $60 strike, and -$8 confuses payoff, which for a long option can never be negative, with profit, which nets out the premium originally paid and is a separate calculation.'),
  ('D24', 'Derivatives', 'Contingent Claims', 'Long call profit (max loss)', 'An investor buys a call option for a premium of $5, with a strike price of $50. At expiration, the underlying stock is trading at $47. The investor''s profit or loss on this position is:', '["$0, since the option is out-of-the-money and therefore worthless","-$3, since the stock finished $3 below the strike price","-$5, a loss equal to the full premium paid, since the option expires out-of-the-money"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Because the stock''s expiration price of $47 is below the $50 strike, the call''s payoff is max(0, 47 - 50) = $0, and the investor''s profit is payoff minus the premium paid: 0 - 5 = -$5, a loss equal to the entire premium, which is the maximum possible loss on a long option position. The $0 figure correctly identifies the payoff but stops there without netting out the premium already spent, and the -$3 figure instead treats the $3 gap between stock price and strike as if it were the loss, skipping the payoff step entirely.'),
  ('D25', 'Derivatives', 'Contingent Claims', 'Short call payoff', 'An investor writes (sells) a call option with a strike price of $90. At expiration, the underlying stock is trading at $97. The payoff to the short call position is:', '["$7","-$7","$0"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The payoff to a short call is the mirror image of the long call''s payoff: -max(0, S_T - X) = -max(0, 97 - 90) = -$7, a loss to the writer because the holder will exercise and buy a $97 stock for only $90. The $7 figure drops the negative sign, reporting the long call holder''s payoff instead of the writer''s, and $0 would only be correct if the stock had finished at or below the $90 strike, leaving the option unexercised.'),
  ('D26', 'Derivatives', 'Contingent Claims', 'Short call profit', 'An investor writes a call option for a premium of $4, with a strike price of $65. At expiration, the underlying stock is trading at $71. The writer''s profit or loss on this position is:', '["$4, the full premium received, since the option writer always keeps the premium","-$6, the full payoff owed on the option, ignoring the premium collected","-$2, the premium received minus the payoff owed on the option"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The short call''s profit is the premium received minus the payoff owed: 4 - max(0, 71 - 65) = 4 - 6 = -$2. The $4 figure forgets that the writer still owes the $6 payoff once the option finishes in-the-money, incorrectly assuming the premium is kept free and clear regardless of what happens to the stock, and -$6 reports the payoff owed alone without crediting the $4 premium the writer received upfront to enter the position.'),
  ('D27', 'Derivatives', 'Contingent Claims', 'Long put payoff', 'An investor holds a long put option with a strike price of $55. At expiration, the underlying stock is trading at $48. The payoff to the long put is:', '["-$7","$0","$7"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The payoff to a long put at expiration is max(0, X - S_T) = max(0, 55 - 48) = $7, since the holder can sell at $55 a stock worth only $48. The -$7 figure incorrectly assigns a negative sign to a long option''s payoff, which can never be negative, and $0 would only be correct if the stock had finished at or above the $55 strike, leaving the put unexercised.'),
  ('D28', 'Derivatives', 'Contingent Claims', 'Long put profit', 'An investor buys a put option for a premium of $3, with a strike price of $80. At expiration, the underlying stock is trading at $71. The investor''s profit on this position is:', '["$6","$9","$12"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The put''s payoff is max(0, X - S_T) = max(0, 80 - 71) = $9, and profit nets out the $3 premium paid: 9 - 3 = $6. The $9 figure is the payoff before the premium is deducted, not the profit, and $12 mistakenly adds the premium to the payoff instead of subtracting it.'),
  ('D29', 'Derivatives', 'Contingent Claims', 'Short put payoff', 'An investor writes (sells) a put option with a strike price of $35. At expiration, the underlying stock is trading at $29. The payoff to the short put position is:', '["$6","$0","-$6"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The payoff to a short put is the mirror image of the long put''s payoff: -max(0, X - S_T) = -max(0, 35 - 29) = -$6, a loss to the writer, who is obligated to buy at $35 a stock now worth only $29. The $6 figure drops the negative sign, reporting the long put holder''s payoff instead of the writer''s, and $0 would only be correct if the stock had finished at or above the $35 strike, leaving the put unexercised.'),
  ('D30', 'Derivatives', 'Contingent Claims', 'Short put profit (max gain)', 'An investor writes a put option for a premium of $2.50, with a strike price of $45. At expiration, the underlying stock is trading at $45, exactly at the strike. The writer''s profit on this position is:', '["$0, since the option expires exactly at the money and has no value either way","$2.50, the full premium received, since the put expires with zero payoff owed","-$2.50, since the writer must still buy the stock at the strike price"]'::jsonb, 1, 'At expiration the stock equals the strike, so the put''s payoff is max(0, 45 - 45) = $0 and the writer owes nothing beyond that; profit is the premium received minus the zero payoff: 2.50 - 0 = $2.50, the maximum possible gain for a short put. The $0 figure correctly notes the payoff is zero but forgets the writer keeps the premium regardless, and -$2.50 wrongly assumes the writer is still forced to buy the stock at a loss even though at-the-money exercise produces no payoff at all.'),
  ('D31', 'Derivatives', 'Contingent Claims', 'Long call reverse breakeven', 'An investor buys a call option with a strike price of $100 for a premium of $6. At what stock price at expiration would the investor''s profit equal exactly $14?', '["$106, since breakeven occurs at the strike plus the premium","$114, since the target profit is added directly to the strike price","$120, since the payoff must equal the target profit plus the premium paid"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Profit equals payoff minus premium, so a target profit of $14 requires a payoff of 14 + 6 = $20; since payoff is S_T - 100 for an in-the-money call, S_T must equal 100 + 20 = $120. The $106 figure is this option''s ordinary breakeven price (strike plus premium, where profit is exactly zero), not the price needed for a $14 profit, and $114 adds the $14 target profit directly to the strike price while forgetting to also add back the $6 premium the payoff must first cover.'),
  ('D32', 'Derivatives', 'Contingent Claims', 'Max loss across positions', 'An investor is considering four separate option positions on the same underlying stock: a long call, a short call, a long put, and a short put. Which statement about the maximum possible loss on these positions is correct?', '["The long call and long put each have unlimited maximum loss, since the stock price can move against the holder without limit","The short call has a maximum loss limited to the premium received, the same as the long call''s maximum loss","The short call has a theoretically unlimited maximum loss, while the long call''s maximum loss is limited to the premium paid"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A long option''s maximum loss is always capped at the premium paid, since the holder simply lets a worthless option expire, but a short call''s maximum loss is theoretically unlimited because the underlying stock price can rise without bound, forcing the writer to buy at an ever-higher price to deliver at the fixed strike. Assigning unlimited loss to the long call and long put reverses this entirely, and equating the short call''s maximum loss with the long call''s maximum loss incorrectly treats them as the same, when in fact the two are opposites: one capped at the premium, the other theoretically uncapped.'),
  ('D33', 'Derivatives', 'Swaps', 'Interest rate swap definition', 'A plain vanilla interest rate swap is best described as an agreement in which two counterparties:', '["Exchange a stream of fixed-rate interest payments for a stream of floating-rate interest payments on a common notional principal","Exchange the notional principal amounts at the start of the agreement and swap them back at maturity","Each pay a floating rate tied to a different benchmark, with no fixed-rate leg involved"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A plain vanilla interest rate swap exchanges a fixed-rate payment stream for a floating-rate payment stream, both calculated on the same notional principal amount over the life of the agreement. The notional principal itself is never actually exchanged between the parties, unlike in a currency swap, and a plain vanilla swap by definition has one fixed-rate leg and one floating-rate leg, not two floating legs.'),
  ('D34', 'Derivatives', 'Swaps', 'Notional principal mechanics', 'In a plain vanilla interest rate swap, the notional principal:', '["Is never exchanged between the counterparties; it is used only to calculate each period''s interest payments","Is exchanged by both parties at initiation and exchanged back at maturity","Is paid entirely by the fixed-rate payer to the floating-rate payer at initiation"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The notional principal in an interest rate swap is purely a reference amount used to calculate the size of each period''s fixed and floating interest payments; it never actually changes hands between the two counterparties. Believing the principal itself is exchanged confuses an interest rate swap with a currency swap, where principal amounts (in two different currencies) genuinely are exchanged at initiation and again at maturity.'),
  ('D35', 'Derivatives', 'Swaps', 'Net settlement', 'At each settlement date of a plain vanilla interest rate swap, the two counterparties typically:', '["Each pay their full fixed or floating obligation in full to the other party, gross of any offset","Exchange only the notional principal, with interest obligations settled separately at year-end","Exchange only the net difference between the fixed-rate and floating-rate payments owed"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Interest rate swaps are typically net settled: only the difference between what the fixed-rate payer and floating-rate payer owe each other changes hands, rather than both parties exchanging their full gross payment. Paying the full gross obligation in both directions would achieve the same net economic result but is not how these swaps are conventionally settled, and the notional principal, as a purely notional reference amount, is never exchanged at all, at any settlement date.'),
  ('D36', 'Derivatives', 'Swaps', 'Pay-fixed swap hedge', 'A company has issued floating-rate debt and is concerned that interest rates will rise, increasing its interest expense. To hedge this exposure, the company would most likely:', '["Enter a swap to receive fixed and pay floating, doubling its exposure to rising rates","Enter a swap to pay fixed and receive floating, converting its floating-rate exposure into a fixed effective rate","Enter a swap to pay floating and receive floating on two different benchmark rates"]'::jsonb, 1, 'By entering a swap in which it pays a fixed rate and receives a floating rate, the company uses the floating receipts to offset the floating-rate payments owed on its own debt, leaving it with a fixed net interest cost regardless of where rates move. Receiving fixed and paying floating would instead add a second floating-rate exposure on top of the existing debt, compounding rather than hedging the risk, and swapping floating for floating on two benchmarks addresses basis risk, not the directional exposure to rising rates described here.'),
  ('D37', 'Derivatives', 'Swaps', 'Swap net settlement calculation', 'In a $10,000,000 notional interest rate swap, the fixed rate is 5.0% and the floating rate for the current settlement period is 4.2%, both applied quarterly (one-fourth of the annual rate). The net settlement payment for this period is:', '["$80,000, paid by the fixed-rate payer to the floating-rate payer","$20,000, paid by the fixed-rate payer to the floating-rate payer","$20,000, paid by the floating-rate payer to the fixed-rate payer"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The net settlement is the notional times the rate difference times the quarterly time fraction: 10,000,000 x (5.0% - 4.2%) x 0.25 = 10,000,000 x 0.008 x 0.25 = $20,000. Because the fixed rate exceeds the floating rate this period, the fixed-rate payer owes more than it receives and pays the $20,000 net difference to the floating-rate payer; the $80,000 figure forgets to apply the quarterly time fraction, using the full annual rate difference instead of one-fourth of it, and reversing the direction of payment ignores that it is the side owing the larger gross amount that makes the net payment.'),
  ('D38', 'Derivatives', 'Swaps', 'Swap net settlement direction', 'In a $5,000,000 notional interest rate swap, the fixed rate is 3.6% and the floating rate for the current settlement period is 4.4%, both applied semiannually (one-half of the annual rate). The net settlement payment for this period is:', '["$20,000, paid by the floating-rate payer to the fixed-rate payer","$20,000, paid by the fixed-rate payer to the floating-rate payer","$40,000, paid by the floating-rate payer to the fixed-rate payer"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The net settlement is the notional times the rate difference times the semiannual time fraction: 5,000,000 x (4.4% - 3.6%) x 0.5 = 5,000,000 x 0.008 x 0.5 = $20,000. Because the floating rate exceeds the fixed rate this period, the floating-rate payer owes more than it receives and pays the $20,000 net difference to the fixed-rate payer; reversing the direction ignores which side''s gross obligation is larger, and $40,000 applies the full annual rate difference without scaling it down for the semiannual settlement period.'),
  ('D39', 'Derivatives', 'Swaps', 'Swap value at initiation', 'At the inception of a plain vanilla interest rate swap, the fixed rate is typically set so that the swap''s value to each counterparty is:', '["Positive to the fixed-rate payer and negative to the floating-rate payer, reflecting the fixed payer''s certainty","Approximately zero to both counterparties, consistent with no-arbitrage pricing","Equal to the full notional principal amount for whichever side benefits from the initial rate environment"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Just as a forward contract''s price is set so the contract has zero value to either party at initiation, the fixed rate on a new swap is set so that the present value of the expected fixed payments equals the present value of the expected floating payments, making the swap''s initial value approximately zero to both sides. Neither side starts with a built-in advantage, and the notional principal is never paid to either party at all, since it exists only as a reference amount for calculating interest.'),
  ('D40', 'Derivatives', 'Swaps', 'Swap-hedged effective borrowing cost', 'A company has $20,000,000 of floating-rate debt on which it pays the floating reference rate. To hedge, it enters a $20,000,000 notional swap in which it pays a fixed rate of 4.5% and receives the floating reference rate, settled annually. During the year, the floating reference rate averages 5.3%. Accounting for both the interest paid on its debt and the net swap settlement, the company''s effective annual interest cost as a percentage of notional is:', '["4.5%, since the swap''s fixed rate exactly offsets the floating-rate debt payment regardless of the floating rate''s level","5.3%, since the swap has no effect once the floating rate differs from the fixed rate","9.8%, since the debt payment and the swap''s fixed-rate payment are both owed in full"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The company pays 5.3% on its floating-rate debt but receives 5.3% floating from the swap, which exactly cancels, leaving only the 4.5% fixed rate it pays on the swap as its net cost, regardless of where the floating rate actually lands, which is the entire purpose of the pay-fixed, receive-floating hedge. Reporting 5.3% ignores the swap entirely, as if the hedge did nothing, and 9.8% incorrectly adds the debt''s floating payment to the swap''s fixed payment instead of netting the offsetting floating legs against each other first.'),
  ('D41', 'Derivatives', 'No-Arbitrage and Put-Call Parity', 'No-arbitrage principle', 'The no-arbitrage principle in asset pricing states that:', '["Any two assets with different cash flows must sell for the same price","Two portfolios with identical future payoffs in every state of the world must have the same price today","Prices can differ across markets as long as transaction costs are positive"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The no-arbitrage principle, also called the law of one price, holds that if two portfolios produce identical payoffs in every possible future scenario, they must trade at the same price today; if they did not, an arbitrageur could buy the cheaper one, sell the more expensive one, and lock in a riskless profit. Requiring equal prices for assets with different cash flows is wrong on its face, since dissimilar payoffs have no reason to share a price at all, and claiming prices can simply differ across markets describes exactly the mispricing that no-arbitrage forbids from persisting, not a condition it permits.'),
  ('D42', 'Derivatives', 'No-Arbitrage and Put-Call Parity', 'Put-call parity relationship', 'For a European call and put on the same underlying stock, with the same strike price and the same expiration date, put-call parity states that:', '["The call premium must always be greater than the put premium, regardless of the strike price","A call plus a risk-free bond paying the strike price at expiration has the same payoff as a put plus the underlying stock","The put premium equals the call premium minus the current stock price, with no other terms involved"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Put-call parity states that C + PV(X) = P + S0: a fiduciary call (a call plus a bond that pays the strike at expiration) produces the exact same payoff at expiration, in every scenario, as a protective put (a put plus the stock itself), so the two portfolios must have the same price today. The relative size of C versus P actually depends on the strike price and the risk-free rate, not a fixed rule, and defining the put as simply the call minus the stock price omits the present value of the strike price entirely, garbling the actual relationship.'),
  ('D43', 'Derivatives', 'No-Arbitrage and Put-Call Parity', 'Solve for call via parity', 'A stock trades at $54. A put option with a strike price of $50 and the same expiration trades for $2.00. The present value of the strike price, discounted at the risk-free rate to expiration, is $49.00. Using put-call parity, the call option''s price should be:', '["$7.00","$3.00","$5.00"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Put-call parity gives C = P + S0 - PV(X) = 2.00 + 54.00 - 49.00 = $7.00. The $3.00 figure subtracts the put premium instead of adding it (54.00 - 49.00 - 2.00), reversing that term''s sign in the parity equation, and $5.00 computes only S0 - PV(X) = 54.00 - 49.00, dropping the put premium term from the equation entirely.'),
  ('D44', 'Derivatives', 'No-Arbitrage and Put-Call Parity', 'Solve for put via parity', 'A stock trades at $38. A call option with a strike price of $40 and the same expiration trades for $3.50. The present value of the strike price, discounted at the risk-free rate to expiration, is $39.20. Using put-call parity, the put option''s price should be:', '["$4.70","$1.20","$2.30"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Put-call parity gives P = C + PV(X) - S0 = 3.50 + 39.20 - 38.00 = $4.70. The $1.20 figure computes PV(X) - S0 = 39.20 - 38.00 alone, dropping the call premium term from the equation entirely, and $2.30 flips the sign on the present-value-of-strike term, computing C - PV(X) + S0 = 3.50 - 39.20 + 38.00 instead of correctly adding PV(X) and subtracting the stock price.'),
  ('D45', 'Derivatives', 'No-Arbitrage and Put-Call Parity', 'Solve for PV(strike) via parity', 'A call option trades at $6.00 and a put option on the same stock, with the same strike price and expiration, trades at $2.50. The stock currently trades at $61.50. Using put-call parity, the present value of the strike price (the risk-free bond component) is:', '["$70.00","$53.00","$58.00"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Rearranging put-call parity, PV(X) = P + S0 - C = 2.50 + 61.50 - 6.00 = $58.00. The $70.00 figure adds the call premium instead of subtracting it (2.50 + 61.50 + 6.00), and $53.00 subtracts both option premiums from the stock price (61.50 - 2.50 - 6.00), when only the call premium should be subtracted while the put premium is added.'),
  ('D46', 'Derivatives', 'No-Arbitrage and Put-Call Parity', 'Synthetic call construction', 'According to put-call parity, a synthetic call option can be created by combining which three positions?', '["Long the underlying stock, long a put, and long a risk-free bond","Short the underlying stock, short a put, and long a risk-free bond paying the strike price","Long a put, long the underlying stock, and short a risk-free bond that pays the strike price at expiration"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Rearranging put-call parity, C = P + S0 - PV(X), so a synthetic call is replicated by going long the put, long the underlying stock, and short a risk-free bond that pays the strike price at expiration (that is, borrowing the present value of the strike). Going long all three positions, including the bond, reverses the sign on the borrowing needed, and shorting both the stock and the put moves in the opposite direction from what the call''s payoff requires.'),
  ('D47', 'Derivatives', 'No-Arbitrage and Put-Call Parity', 'Arbitrage direction identification', 'A stock trades at $40. A European call and put, both with a strike price of $40 and the same expiration, trade at $4.50 and $3.00 respectively. The present value of the strike price is $39.50. Comparing C + PV(X) to P + S0, the correct arbitrage strategy is to:', '["Sell the call, buy the put, buy the stock, and borrow the present value of the strike price, since C + PV(X) is overpriced relative to P + S0","Buy the call, sell the put, sell the stock short, and lend (invest) the present value of the strike price, since C + PV(X) is underpriced relative to P + S0","No arbitrage is available, since both sides of the parity equation are within a few dollars of each other"]'::jsonb, 0, 'C + PV(X) = 4.50 + 39.50 = $44.00 while P + S0 = 3.00 + 40.00 = $43.00, so the fiduciary call combination (call plus bond) is overpriced by $1.00 relative to the protective put combination (put plus stock); the correct arbitrage sells the overpriced combination (write the call and borrow the present value of the strike) and buys the underpriced one (buy the put and the stock), locking in the $1.00 difference today with an exactly offsetting payoff at expiration. Buying the call while selling the put and shorting the stock correctly structures the opposite trade but for the wrong direction of mispricing, and assuming no arbitrage is available wrongly treats a $1.00 gap as too small to matter, when parity requires exact equality and any gap at all is, in principle, a locked-in riskless profit before transaction costs.'),
  ('D48', 'Derivatives', 'No-Arbitrage and Put-Call Parity', 'Arbitrage profit calculation', 'A stock trades at $85. A European call and put, both with a strike price of $85 and the same expiration, trade at $5.20 and $7.70 respectively. The present value of the strike price is $83.10. The riskless arbitrage profit available per set of options, ignoring transaction costs, is closest to:', '["$2.50, the difference between the put premium and the call premium alone","$4.40, the difference between P + S0 and C + PV(X)","$12.90, the sum of the call and put premiums"]'::jsonb, 1, 'P + S0 = 7.70 + 85.00 = $92.70 while C + PV(X) = 5.20 + 83.10 = $88.30, so the protective put combination is overpriced relative to the fiduciary call by 92.70 - 88.30 = $4.40, the riskless profit available by selling the protective put (sell the put, sell the stock) and buying the fiduciary call (buy the call, invest the present value of the strike). The $2.50 figure only compares the two option premiums directly (7.70 - 5.20) and ignores the stock price and present value of the strike entirely, and $12.90 simply adds the two premiums together rather than computing the actual gap between the two sides of the parity equation.'),
  ('D49', 'Derivatives', 'No-Arbitrage and Put-Call Parity', 'True arbitrage conditions', 'An analyst notices that put-call parity appears violated and immediately buys the underpriced combination and sells the overpriced combination, funding the entire trade with borrowed money and holding no other position. For this to be considered a true arbitrage rather than speculation, which condition must hold?', '["The analyst must be confident the mispricing will persist for several weeks before correcting","The analyst must earn a return that exceeds the return available on the overall stock market","The strategy must require no net investment of the analyst''s own capital and produce no risk of loss in any future scenario"]'::jsonb, 2, 'True arbitrage requires both a zero net investment (the position is fully self-financing, as when the entire trade is funded by borrowing) and no risk of loss under any future outcome, since the offsetting legs are constructed to cancel out exactly regardless of where the underlying ends up. Requiring the mispricing to persist for weeks actually describes a risky bet on convergence timing, which is closer to speculation than arbitrage, and comparing the return to the broader stock market is irrelevant, since a genuine arbitrage profit is riskless and locked in immediately, not a relative-performance question.'),
  ('D50', 'Derivatives', 'No-Arbitrage and Put-Call Parity', 'Numeric parity check', 'A stock trades at $95. A call with a strike price of $95 trades at $6.20, and a put with the same strike and expiration trades at $5.00. The present value of the strike price is $93.50. By how much does C + PV(X) differ from P + S0?', '["C + PV(X) is $0.30 less than P + S0","C + PV(X) is $0.30 more than P + S0","C + PV(X) equals P + S0 exactly, so parity holds"]'::jsonb, 0, 'C + PV(X) = 6.20 + 93.50 = $99.70, while P + S0 = 5.00 + 95.00 = $100.00, so the fiduciary call side is $0.30 less than the protective put side, a small but real parity violation before transaction costs. Reversing which side is larger simply flips the direction of the $0.30 gap, and claiming the two sides are exactly equal ignores that $99.70 and $100.00 are not the same number, however close they may look at a glance.');
